  "query_preview": "..\n\nOur stretch of outstanding mid November weather continues this \nafternoon through the bulk of the weekend as the Sunday cold front \nmoves through with slightly later afternoon timing. Highs this \nafternoon in the mid 60s most locations are a solid 20 degrees above\nnormal and within a few degrees of records for the 20th. Record \nhighs are also vulnerable Saturday and Sunday as listed in the \nClimate section below. \n\nAn ideal combination of dry and mild surface high pressure, and a \nprogressive mid/upper level ridge, has clouds limited to only some \nthin cirrus this afternoon. Light SW wind is just getting started \nand has a more beneficial affect on temperatures tonight with some \ninterruption of radiational cooling. Morning lows only in the 40s \nonce again jump start readings for Saturday, along with plentiful \nsunshine, leading to highs in the mid 60s to near 70. It is \nimpressive warmth despite model soundings that indicate relatively \nshallow boundary layer growth to around 950 mb during the afternoon, \nwhich makes sense for this time of year. Even so, temperatures are \nstill able to make a run at records for Nov 21st. \n\nThe warm air is firmly established ahead of broad Plains low \npressure with a north/south frontal zone that reaches the MS valley \nSaturday night. The leading moisture axis is Gulf enhanced with a \nsharp 850-700 mb theta-e structure in model projections as it enters \nwestern Lower Mi early Sunday morning. The primary surface low in \nnorthern Ontario pulls the cold front through Lower Mi late Sunday \nand Sunday night with at least broken coverage of showers given the \nboost from the southern stream mid level short wave. Clouds end up \nbeing more of challenge for afternoon high temperatures, however the \nlate afternoon timing keeps another round of near record warmth in \nreach. \n\nIt is then back to reality by Monday as the cold front brings a \nsharp temperature transition for Thanksgiving week. Readings end up \njust a few degrees below normal, but the wide swing from near record \nwarmth makes for a dramatic 25 to 30 degree drop. Low temperatures \nin the lower to mid 20s also represent the first hard freeze of the \nseason and lead to highs only in the upper 30s Tuesday. The \npotential for rain/snow is limited by a muted NW flow lake effect \nresponse Monday and Tuesday, and assuming SE Mi is only grazed by \nthe Wednesday/Thanksgiving system tracking from the Ohio valley to \nmid Atlantic coast. This system is one to monitor as long range \ndeterministic models offer a wide range of solutions on the \nmid/upper level pattern during late next week.",
